  "id": "COMPOUND:537 - PROTEIN:1380",
  "verbalization": "Acetohydroxamic acid is implicated in the target relationship with ATP synthase subunit a through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that acetohydroxamic acid acts as a target for several entities, including 72 kDa type IV collagenase and 6-hydroxy-2-(hydroxymethyl)-1-methoxy-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyanthracene-9,10-dione, which in turn target ATP synthase subunit a. These pathways highlight a potential mechanism where acetohydroxamic acid may influence the activity of these intermediates, which then interact with ATP synthase subunit a. The recurring involvement of ATP synthase subunit a as a target across different pathways suggests a possible role in metabolic processes, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains unclear. The integration of these evidence strands supports the hypothesis that acetohydroxamic acid may modulate ATP synthase activity through a series of intermediate targets, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct relationship.",
